WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.680
In this video, we're going to go
through the process of Pinning

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.360
a data set to Posit
Connect using the pins package.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.830
So over here on
the left hand side,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.900
we have a pins demo
R script, and we're

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.180
going to walk through
all of these steps.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.270
The first thing we'll do
is install the pins package

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.090
and then load the pins
package into our environment.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.680
So I'll highlight these lines
of code and I'll hit Run.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.860
Now, in order to use pins, you
first have to define a board.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.450
And so for this
workflow, we're going

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.700
to use Posit Connect
as our board.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.540
The first thing we need to
do is register our board.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.440
So the easiest way to do that is
to go to tools, global options

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.630
and select publishing.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.360
So the top of my
screen I go to tools,

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.850
all the way to the bottom,
we have global options.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.000
And towards the bottom,
we have publishing.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.850
Currently, I do not have any
Posit Connect instances

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.790
registered, so we're going
to connect to a new one.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.120
So I'll click Connect
here in the top, right?

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.000
We're going to choose
Posit Connect,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.520
and here is where you
want to provide the URL

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.470
of your Connect instance.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:16.860
I'll select next and we get this
pop up menu to connect to R

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.010
that all looks
good will connect.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:25.340
I can close out of this
and then connect account.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:25.840
All right.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.340
So we've now connected to our
instance of Posit Connect.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.080
I can apply these
changes and hit.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.120
OK all right.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.750
So now that we've
registered our Posit Connect

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.320
and since we first need to
define this connect instance

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.440
as our board, and to do that,
we're going to use the board,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.290
connect, function
from the pins package.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.570
And we will call
this my board, so

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.010
let me run this line of code.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.860
And you can see at the bottom
connecting, to Posit Connect

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.400
and there's the URL
for my connect instance

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.850
and now we can actually
pin the data set.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.090
So in this example, we're going
to use the pin write function

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.930
from the pins package and we're
going to pin the mtcars

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.370
data set.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.530
We're going to pin it to
my connect instance, which

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.840
we registered in
the previous step.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.040
We're going to call it
Mtcars data set.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.740
You can call it
whatever you want.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.130
Now we're going to save
this pin as a CSV file,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.480
so let's highlight all this.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.300
Will hit run, at the
bottom of the screen

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.590
You can see writing to pin Ryan.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.290
It's me, mtcars data set.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.250
And now we can actually switch
over to Posit Connect

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.520
and I can show you what a
pinned data set looks like.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.790
So here's the pin data set
the mtcars data set.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.770
You can actually get a
snippet of what the data

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:51.480
set looks like right here and
you can actually scroll across.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.310
But most importantly,
with pins, you

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.520
want to know how to pull this
pin into your own workflow,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.430
into your own script,
whatever you're developing,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.460
and it provides the
code and how to do that.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.380
So we're going to take
this code and come back

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.840
to Posit Workbench,
see RStudio IDE, within

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.670
Posit Workbench
here, and we're

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.460
going to read in
that pin data set.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.790
So we'll use the pin read
function from the pins package.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.830
We're going to again
let pins know this

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.400
is the board I want to use.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.650
And I'm going to
pull in this pin.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.690
That's my name, the
Mtcars dataset.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:29.610
So let's run that
and then you'll

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:34.200
see here my console here
is the Printed out pins,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.750
pin mtcars,
data set, and that

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.580
is how you pin a data
set to Posit Connect.

